the  world who run this stuff are  non-technical so they didn't really like  math themselves they didn't really  understand it and now then in a  situation of trying to tell everyone  it's good for them she doesn't work too  well I mean an example from England I  was like you know is where they insisted  long division was a very important thing  for everyone in primary school to know  now whether or not you agree with that  and I certainly don't particularly in  that case  but it's terrible marketing it's like  you're telling people who go through  primary school the most exciting thing  that the government's decided to  highlight about primary maths is that  you can do long division this doesn't  seem like a great recipe for in  confusing people and it's even worse it  they do it because they think that some  some parents will be very excited about  forcing their kids to learn this and  maybe there are some parents who want to  you know get out a whip and whip them  into doing long division but i think the  number in most places is actually rather  small compared to the number who just  like their kids to know how to function  well in the in the real world so there's  a real problem with sort of math  marketing and i think part of that is  presenting exciting things you can do  with math things that are fun like  cheating and playing games and you know  making sure you can outdo your parents  and things like this and I think these  are all sort of important thing so we've  been trying to improve the way in which  we present some of the issues around  maths and I don't we totally succeeded  with that but I think we've got some  sort of message I'm around so another  problem in making a change like this is  you've got to mesh with today's  educational environment um you've got to  train teachers you've got to get  expectations and skills and delivery  right and confidence confidence is  really important you've also got to get  technology that works and you've got to  set up schools and so forth that's the  reality one of the problems in these  discussions is that people get so  obsessed with the reality of what they  have to do next term or next year but  they completely forget the vision the  vision just sort of flies out of the  space and so you end up with a zero  vision process and in fact I wrote a  blog post about evidence and the role of  evidence last week on my personal site  because i realize that often when you  talk about evidence led education reform  what it means is i mean it doesn't mean  randomized control trials i mean the  good thing i think in using evidence is  make you know gasps you guess what you  want to do you build the thing you then  test it and you don't see whether it  seems to work better against criteria  what happens with drugs what evidence  that  education has typically meant is you're  not allowed to build anything unless you  can prove that it fits with educational  evidence from the past right that is not  the way innovation can ever happen in  any serious way incremental it can  happen but no big innovation can ever  happen if that's what you believe has to  happen first then indeed it doesn't work  anywhere any innovation around the world  and anything outside education are in  won't work if you assume education  always has to be led by the evidence of  what's happened in the past and there's  a great Fineman the physicist Fineman  has a very nice bit when he's explaining  how science works which I quote in that  blog post which is kind of nice so  what's our progress well we found a  computer-based maths a number of years  ago for this and I think we are seeing  in some countries the discourse changing  you know there is a star to people  questioning subject we started building  a curriculum and we're making some quite  good progress with that and I think  we've built a whole bunch of frameworks  around that as to you know how do you  think through what should be in a new  curriculum how do you think through  what's real and what isn't real what all  the things should go in we haven't  succeeded everywhere there but I think  we have succeed a long way I mean the  thing I would say and a lot of those are  things like activities chapters topics  modules and modalities or the sort of  framework elements that build up  computer-based mass modules are in a  sense the set of problems that people  want to solve and these the ways that  they get solved our first major place is  Estonia where we have implemented a  pilot probability and statistics creek  and I'm pleased to say the group from  Estonia is here which is great has been  working very much on this and I'm sure  we'll be happy to talk to anyone else  from other countries who has have  questions about it we've also made an  early start in Sweden and Africa on  changing some of these things and  there's interest from many other  countries around the world it's slow  going to get these things to happen  because governments and things move  slowly things in education in general  move very slowly but i think we are we  are making very good progress and some  of the technology this conference will  improve that quite a lot so what do we  have to do well we've got to do the rest  of the math curriculum I think math one  way to think about maths I mean there  are various ways to categorize it I  think there are sort of five categories  we need to hit there's data science  probability and statistics and data  science there is geometry which i think  is a modern area there's modeling  there's what i would call architecture  of math which is kind of the underlying  structures that you need to know and  there's a fifth one that i have lost for  the moment but it's there somewhere  it'll come back to me in a second anyway  there are five our image processing  signal processing was the one I was  looking for and so these are sort of  major modern areas of math maybe there's  some others that you can suggest as 